<p>
    EMUUROM is a game about ramming your head into every corner of a maze and finding every collectible.
</p>
<p>
    
</p>
<p>
    Like many games of its type, EMUUROM features a secret writing system for the player to decipher.
    <span class="spoiler">
        Like Tunic, the system is not a simple one-to-one mapping of letters to symbols, so it was a fun puzzle.
        However, the language encoded is not English, but rather a particular dialect of Finnish, spelled phonetically. 
        Google translate struggles on it, even when the messages are perfectly deciphered.
        And even translated, most messages don't lead anywhere. 
        The few that actually encode secrets are dead-ends.
        I guess this must be what it feels like to work with the Tunic language as a non-native English speaker.</span>
    Overall, the secret language was a bit disappointing.
</p>
<p>
    The game is built within the <a href="https://tic80.com/">TIC-80</a> virtual console,
    which imposes strict limitations on the assets. 
    The graphics are limited to 16 colors on a 240x136 display,
    and the sounds are similarly constrained.
    It's a cool concept, and constraints breed creativity, but honestly, the severe limitations were felt.
    The chiptune music in particular was more annoying than catchy.
    It seems there is a difference between a game that is merely retro-<em>themed</em> that makes full use of modern hardware,
    and a game that is genuinely capped.
</p>
<p>
    EMUUROM asks the player to explore every detail of the world to find all its secrets. The
    problem
    is, the level design and game mechanics are not fun by themselves.
</p>
<p>
    EMUUROM is a time-consuming scavenger hunt. There are a couple epiphany moments. One in
    particular
    was really cool.
</p>

